{"groups":[{"members":["c1","c2"],"primary":"c1","title":"Cartwright Act: ban on use or distribution of common pricing algorithms (AB 325)","requirement":"It is unlawful to use or distribute a common pricing algorithm as part of a contract, combination or conspiracy to restrain trade, or to use or distribute one while coercing another person to adopt its recommended price or commercial term for the same or similar products or services in California. A 'common pricing algorithm' is any methodology, including software or other technology, used by two or more persons that uses competitor data to recommend, align, stabilize, set or otherwise influence a price or commercial term.","key_value":"Unlawful to use or distribute a common pricing algorithm (a) as part of a restraint-of-trade conspiracy, or (b) where the person coerces others to adopt its recommended price or commercial term","status":"in_force","effective_date":"2026-01-01","end_date":null,"citation":"Cal. Bus. & Prof. Code § 16729 (added by Stats. 2025, ch. 338 (AB 325))","coverage_conditions":"Applies to any person, other than the end consumer, who uses or distributes a common pricing algorithm for products or services in California. The law covers all industries, so it reaches residential rent-setting, but the statute does not name rentals. AB 325 also amends B&P Code § 16756.1 so that a Cartwright Act complaint need only plead facts making a contract, combination or conspiracy plausible, without excluding the possibility of independent action.","exemptions":"By definition, it does not cover algorithms that are not used by two or more persons or that do not use competitor data, such as single-firm tools using only the business's own data.","coverage_from":"c1","conflict_note":null}],"dropped":[]}
